[DIAGNOSIS]:
The error arises from the use of an extra '|' (bar) in the expression:
(one c: classificationsH | all c2: classificationsH | higherOrEqualClearance[c, c2] | c)
on line 857. In Alloy 6, the comprehension syntax (one ... | ... ) only allows a single '|' to separate bound variables from the body; using a second '|' is illegal. The correct way to express "the unique c such that all c2: classificationsH have higherOrEqualClearance[c, c2]" is to use the quantified constraint within the body (after the first '|'), and then return c directly as part of the comprehension, not as a second bar-separated expression.

[FIX INTENT]:
Correct all 'let highestX = (one c: ... | ... | c)' expressions to use only one '|' and ensure the body returns c when the constraint holds.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: Locate all lines in EmergencyBypassFIFOScenario (lines 857, 861, 865, and in the wrapper predicate) that use 'let highestX = (one c: ... | all c2: ... | higherOrEqualClearance[c, c2] | c)' or similar.
- Step 2: For each, replace '(one c: S | all c2: S | P[c, c2] | c)' with '(one c: S | all c2: S | P[c, c2])'. The expression after the last bar is invalid; the comprehension already binds c.
- Step 3: Ensure that the body of the comprehension after the first '|' is '(all c2: S | P[c, c2])' (i.e., the unique c such that for all c2, P[c, c2] holds).
- Step 4: Do this for all similar let-bindings in the EmergencyBypassFIFOScenario predicate and the EmergencyBypassFIFOScenarioWithCoverage wrapper.

[RATIONALE]:
This fix uses valid Alloy 6 comprehension syntax, avoiding the illegal multiple-bar construct, and is a different approach from previous failed attempts by directly restructuring the comprehension body to select c by constraint rather than by a trailing projection.

[LESSON]:
Always use only one '|' in Alloy 6 comprehensions; the body can be any formula (including quantifiers and constraints), but additional bars are not allowed.
